From ce6657e8ddd5ae019b2f0637464b93847912d190 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:01:56 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: Dolby Vision metadata made from HDR10+
 metadata

For a display that takes Dolby Vision and not HDR10+, the dynamic metadata
of an HDR10+ video can be translated into what an RPU of profile 8.1 says
of the same picture: an identity mapping (the HDR10 picture is not
changed), BT.2020 PQ, the mastering display as the source range, and a
level 1 block (min, max and average of the scene) from the HDR10+
statistics (SMPTE ST 2094-40:2020 8.3 to 8.5). The rule and the profile
8.1 defaults follow dovi_tool's generator (MIT, read as data), with two
differences, measured:

- the average. dovi_tool takes PQ of AverageMaxRGB, the PQ of the linear
  mean, where ST 2094-10:2021 6.1 defines the mean of maxRGB in PQ. On 166
  pictures of four test streams, against the level 1 computed on the
  pictures themselves, that is 112 codes too high on average (up to 512).
  Here the mean in PQ is taken over the quantile function that the
  percentiles give, linear in PQ between them; between the 95th and the
  last (made at 99.98 percent, ST 2094-40 8.5.3 and 8.5.4), where the
  points are farthest apart, it is shaped so that its linear mean is the
  AverageMaxRGB that HDR10+ carries exactly: 11 codes off on average;
- the max. dovi_tool takes the highest distribution value, the 99.98th
  percentile, which clips the pixels above it (37 codes below the
  level 1 of ST 2094-10 on average). Here it is PQ of the highest MaxSCL,
  which clips none (45 codes above, the level 1 being of the reduced
  pixel set, 2x2 averages).

The entries at 5 and 10 percent of ApplicationVersion 1 are not
percentiles (8.5.4: 0 and 0.00255) and are left out.

The min is 0, as dovi_tool
makes it: HDR10+ has none. The clamps are dovi_tool's for CM v2.9 (the
max at least 2081, the average at least 819), which it also applies to
Dolby's own CM XML when it makes RPUs of them. CM v2.9 only: no trims and
no CM v4.0 blocks, which HDR10+ has nothing to make from.

dvbridge_hdr10plus_next() makes the metadata of each picture of a
stream: once a scene (scenes told as hdr10plus_tool tells them,
dvbridge_hdr10plus_same_scene()), with a scene refresh on the first
picture of each. A picture without HDR10+ metadata of its own, or with
metadata that describe no distribution, gets the last metadata as a
repeated RPU, which a decoder counts as a missing one.

Measured with the bridge on a software OpenGL ES, with an HDR10+ stream
made from a test stream's base layer: in both ways of drawing, the luma of
the transport of certified source devices is the decoded luma times 4 in
all 165 million samples, and so is the chroma of the rows it is sited on;
a luma mapping off by 1/4096 is seen in every sample. The level 1 block
is carried as made.
